.TH PROTOCOLS.CFG 5 "Version 4.3.30:  4 Sep 2019" "Xymon"
.SH NAME
protocols.cfg \- Configuration of TCP network services

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP "\fB$XYMONHOME/etc/protocols.cfg\fR"
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBprotocols.cfg\fR contains definitions of how
.I xymonnet(1)
should test a TCP-based network service (i.e. all common
network services except HTTP and DNS).
For each service, a simple dialogue can be defined to
check that the service is functioning normally, and optional
flags determine if the service has e.g. a banner or requires
SSL- or telnet-style handshaking to be tested.

.SH FILE FORMAT
protocols.cfg is a text file. A simple service definition for
the SMTP service would be this:
.br
.sp
.nf
\&   [smtp]
\&      expect "220" until "220 "
\&      send "ehlo xymonnet\er\en"
\&      expect "250" until "250 "
\&      options banner
.fi
.sp
This defines a service called "smtp". The lines are performed in the order
they are written, which is the order of the conversation: xymonnet waits for
a greeting beginning with "220", sends EHLO, and checks that the answer
begins with "250". Any data returned by the service (a so-called "banner")
will be recorded and included in the status message.
.sp
The order matters, and it is the only thing here that does. A service whose
server speaks first is written with \fBexpect\fR first; one that says
nothing until it is asked \-\- clamd and spamd, for instance \-\- is
written with \fBsend\fR first. Writing before the server has greeted is a
protocol violation in SMTP, and Postfix 3.9 and later reject the session for
it.
.sp
A definition may hold as many steps as the conversation needs. Only the
classic shapes \-\- a lone \fBsend\fR, a lone \fBexpect\fR, or a
\fBsend\fR followed by an \fBexpect\fR \-\- use the single-exchange
probe they have always used.
.sp
For services that require ALPN protocol negotiation (such as IMAPS multiplexed
by Nginx on the same port as HTTPS), you can define:
.br
.sp
.nf
\&   [imaps443]
\&      options ssl,alpn=imap
\&      port 443
.fi
.sp
The full set of commands available for the protocols.cfg file are:

.IP "[NAME]"
Define the name of the TCP service, which will also be the column-name
in the resulting display on the test status. If multiple tests share
a common definition (e.g. ssh, ssh1 and ssh2 are tested identically),
you may list these in a single "[ssh|ssh1|ssh2]" definition,
separating each service-name with a pipe-sign.

.IP "\fBsend\fR \fISTRING\fR"
.IP "\fBexpect\fR \fISTRING\fR"
Defines the strings to send to the service after a connection is
established, and the response that is expected. Either of these
may be omitted, in which case
.I xymonnet(1)
will simply not send any data, or match a response against anything.

An \fBexpect\fR may say where the reply ENDS:
.br
.sp
.nf
\&      expect "250" until "250 "
.fi
.sp
Without \fBuntil\fR one read is taken for the whole reply, which is wrong
for every protocol that answers on several lines: SMTP and FTP continue with
"250\-" and finish with "250 ", NNTP ends a block with ".", IMAP ends with
the command tag. Naming the terminator reads to the end of the reply however
it is split across packets. A greeting that arrives in two packets would
otherwise have its tail matched as the answer to the next command, and a
healthy server would be reported down.

The send- and expect-strings use standard escaping for non-printable
characters. "\er" represents a carriage-return (ASCII 13), "\en"
represents a line-feed (ASCII 10), "\et" represents a TAB (ASCII 8).
Binary data is input as "\exNN" with NN being the hexadecimal value
of the byte.

.IP "\fBstart\fR \fIFEATURE\fR"
Switch on something the CLIENT does. The word after \fBstart\fR names code
in xymonnet rather than anything sent to the server, and is reserved:
\fBtls\fR is the only feature, and naming another is refused when the file
is read.
.sp
\fBstart tls\fR upgrades the connection where the line sits \-\- after the
server has agreed to it, never before:
.br
.sp
.nf
\&   [smtptls]
\&      expect "220" until "220 "
\&      send "ehlo xymonnet\er\en"
\&      expect "250" until "250 "
\&      send "starttls\er\en"
\&      expect "220"
\&      start tls
.fi
.sp
Everything before the line is plaintext and everything after it is
encrypted, on the same socket. This is what SMTP on 25, submission on 587
and IMAP on 143 do, and it is the only way any of them reaches a
certificate: the \fBssl\fR option means TLS from the first byte, which is a
different port and a different service. The certificate is read from the
upgraded session, so a service that begins in plaintext reaches the
\fBsslcert\fR column with its expiry checked.
.sp
Two words, because the action is not protocol-specific. FTP spells the
request "AUTH TLS", POP3 "STLS", SMTP and IMAP "STARTTLS" \-\- but what the
client does is the same in each, and the asking is left to a \fBsend\fR,
which is the only line that knows the protocol.
.sp
The \fBssl\fR option is spelled that way for historical reasons; what both
it and \fBstart tls\fR negotiate is TLS.

.IP "\fBport\fR \fINUMBER\fR"
Define the default TCP port-number for this service. If no portnumber
is defined,
.I xymonnet(1)
will attempt to lookup the portnumber in the standard /etc/services
file.

.IP "\fBoptions\fR \fIoption1\fR[,\fIoption2\fR][,\fIoption3\fR]"
Defines test options. The possible options are
.br
.nf
\&   banner - include received data in the status message
\&   ssl - service uses SSL so perform an SSL handshake
\&   telnet - service is telnet, so exchange telnet options
\&   alpn=protocol1,protocol2 - specify ALPN protocols to negotiate during SSL handshake
.fi


.SH FILES
.IP "\fB$XYMONHOME/etc/protocols.cfg\fR"
.SH "SEE ALSO"
xymonnet(1)

